<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Courland Pocket</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> Pocket was the area of western Latvia where German Army Group North, later renamed Army Group Courland, was cut off by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> from October 1944. Repeated Soviet offensives failed to destroy it, and around 200,000 German troops held out until the general surrender in May 1945. Latvian soldiers fought on both sides in the battles there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/courland-pocket.jpg" alt="Courland Pocket" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Drawn by GdrModified by Zocky, Mahahahaneapneap, Julieta39, Claude Zygiel · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eastern_Front_1943-08_to_1944-12.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In June 1941 Army Group North swept through the Baltic states, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> had occupied since June 1940, on its way to Leningrad, which it besieged for more than two years without taking it. After the siege was lifted in January 1944 the army group fell back to the Panther Line. Operation Bagration, launched on 22 June 1944, almost destroyed Army Group Centre by late August and forced Army Group North to retreat through the Baltic states. On 9 October 1944 the 1st Baltic Front reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a> (Klaipėda), cutting the army group off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> in a pocket between Tukums and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Heinz Guderian, Chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">German General Staff</a>, urged an evacuation to strengthen the front in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> ordered the troops to hold out, believing them necessary to protect submarine bases on the Baltic coast. Thirty-three divisions under Feldmarschall Ferdinand Schörner withdrew onto the peninsula, abandoning Riga, which the 3rd Baltic Front took on 13 October. Between 15 October 1944 and 4 April 1945 Soviet forces launched six major offensives. In the battle that began on 21 December, the Latvian 130th Rifle Corps of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> faced the Latvian 19th SS Division.</p><p>On 25 January 1945 the force was renamed Army Group Courland under Colonel-General Lothar Rendulic, and some divisions were evacuated by sea. In seven months the Soviets advanced no more than a few kilometres in difficult terrain and bad weather. Western historians hold that after December 1944 the Soviet command aimed mainly to keep the Germans from escaping; the Russian historian Aleksei Isaev calls Courland a peripheral front for both sides. German losses are estimated at over 150,000, and Grigoriy Krivosheev counts 160,948 Soviet casualties between 16 February and 8 May 1945.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the German Instrument of Surrender of 8 May 1945, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-donitz">Karl Dönitz</a> ordered the last commander, Colonel-General Carl Hilpert, to surrender, but the cut-off troops received the order only on 10 May, making Army Group Courland one of the last major German formations to lay down its arms. The prisoners, Germans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvians">Latvians</a>, were taken to camps in the Soviet interior. Hilpert was executed for war crimes after a trial in Soviet captivity in 1947. The Soviets detained all men aged 16 to 60 and burned tracts of forest to flush out resisters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Courland Pocket” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Second World War Latvians were mobilised by both sides, notably in the Latvian Legion of the Waffen-SS, and fighting continued in the <b>Courland Pocket</b> until May 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>German forces trapped in the <b>Courland Pocket</b> held out until May 1945.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-ostland">Reichskommissariat Ostland</a></li></ul></section>
